lessons.refined:
IN PLAIN WORDS
A component is a function with a capitalized name that returns JSX. You define it once, then render it as a tag, like <ScorePanel />, as many times as you want. Nothing shows until it is rendered.

KEY IDEAS
1. The name must start with a capital letter (ScorePanel, HealthBar). React treats lowercase tags as HTML.
2. The function returns JSX with one root element.
3. Writing <ScorePanel /> inside another component's JSX is "rendering" it.
4. Define once, render many: each <ScorePanel /> is a separate copy.

STEPS
1. Write the component function above App in App.jsx.
2. Return its JSX.
3. Put <ComponentName /> inside App's return.
4. Save and check the page.

CODE
```jsx
function ScorePanel() {
  return (
    <div className="score-panel">
      <h2>Score: 100</h2>
    </div>
  );
}

function App() {
  return (
    <>
      <h1>My HUD</h1>
      <ScorePanel />
      <ScorePanel />
    </>
  );
}

export default App;
```

KEY POINTS
- HTML tags are lowercase (div, h1, button); your components are capitalized.
- A component that is defined but never rendered shows nothing.

IN PLAIN WORDS
Props are data a parent component passes into a child component, written like HTML attributes. The child receives them as one object and usually destructures them: function ProfileCard({ name, role }).

KEY IDEAS
1. <ProfileCard name="Knight" role="Tank" /> becomes the props object { name: "Knight", role: "Tank" }.
2. One component with different props gives different output.
3. Data flows one way: parent → child.
4. Props are read-only inside the child. Use them; don't reassign them.

CODE
```jsx
// src/components/ProfileCard.jsx
function ProfileCard({ name, role }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{name}</h2>
      <p>{role}</p>
    </div>
  );
}
export default ProfileCard;
```

```jsx
// src/App.jsx
import ProfileCard from "./components/ProfileCard.jsx";

function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Party</h1>
      <ProfileCard name="Knight" role="Tank" />
      <ProfileCard name="Mage" role="DPS" />
    </div>
  );
}
export default App;
```

KEY POINTS
- App is the parent, ProfileCard is the child.
- { name, role } in the parameter list is destructuring the props object.
- Strings are passed in quotes; other values (numbers, booleans, variables) go in braces, like hp={40}.

IN PLAIN WORDS
Put the data in an array, use .map() to turn each item into JSX, and place the result in your JSX with { }. Change the array and the UI changes, without touching the JSX.

KEY IDEAS
1. The array holds the data (one object per item).
2. A reusable component shows one item.
3. array.map(item => <Component ... />) runs once per item and returns a new array of JSX elements.
4. {profileCards} inside JSX tells React to render the whole list.
5. Add, remove or edit an object in the array, and the matching card appears, disappears or changes.

STEPS
1. Make the data array.
2. Write the component for one item.
3. map the array to components.
4. Render the mapped list inside the parent's JSX.

CODE
```jsx
const profiles = [
  { name: "Healer", role: "Support", isOnline: true },
  { name: "Mage", role: "DPS", isOnline: false },
  { name: "Test Player", role: "Wizard", isOnline: true },
  { name: "Test Enemy", role: "", isOnline: false },
  { name: "Elementalist", role: "Support/DPS", isOnline: true },
];

function ProfileCard({ name, role, isOnline }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{name || "Unknown User"}</h2>
      {role && <p>{role}</p>}
      <p>{isOnline ? "Online" : "Offline"}</p>
    </div>
  );
}

function App() {
  const profileCards = profiles.map((profile) => (
    <ProfileCard
      name={profile.name}
      role={profile.role}
      isOnline={profile.isOnline}
    />
  ));

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Party</h1>
      {profileCards}
    </div>
  );
}
```

KEY POINTS
- Rule: if it can be listed in an array, it can be rendered with map.
- name || "Unknown User" shows a fallback when name is empty.

NOTE
These examples have no key, so React prints a warning in the console. Lesson 5 fixes that with key={profile.id}; the objects need an id field first.

IN PLAIN WORDS
A default export is imported without braces; a named export is imported with braces. Keep one component per file, name the file after the component, and use the same export style everywhere for that component.

KEY IDEAS
1. export default Button; ↔ import Button from './Button.jsx';
2. export function Button() {...} ↔ import { Button } from './Button.jsx';
3. A file has at most one default export but can have many named exports.
4. Rule: see the word default → no braces. No default → braces.

STEPS
Converting a default export to a named export:
1. Remove export default ProfileCard; at the bottom.
2. Put export in front of the function: export function ProfileCard(props) {...}
3. Change every import from import ProfileCard ... to import { ProfileCard } ...

CODE
```jsx
// Button.jsx (default)
function Button() {
  return <button>Click</button>;
}
export default Button;

// import
import Button from './Button.jsx';
```

```jsx
// Button.jsx (named)
export function Button() {
  return <button>Click</button>;
}

// import
import { Button } from './Button.jsx';
```

KEY POINTS
- Files usually live in src/components/, and the file name matches the component (ProfileCard.jsx → ProfileCard).
- Avoid exporting the same component both ways in one file.
- import { Enemy } fails when the file only has export default Enemy, and import Enemy fails when the file only has export function Enemy().

IN PLAIN WORDS
Style React components with className (never class). Global CSS (index.css) is for the page, fonts and plain tags. A component's CSS file (ProfileCard.css, imported in that component) holds styles for that component. Classes can change with the data using a template literal.

KEY IDEAS
1. className="profile-card" in JSX becomes class="profile-card" in the page.
2. Global CSS: body background, fonts, generic tags like h1 and button.
3. Component CSS: rules for one component, written with its own class names.
4. More specific selectors win: .profile-card h2 beats h2.
5. Base class + status class: one class for the shared look, extra classes for states (online, offline, boss).

STEPS
Using AI-drafted CSS:
1. Describe the look and list your class names.
2. Paste the draft into a scratch area, not straight into the project.
3. Delete what you don't need.
4. Make sure the class names match the JSX.
5. Adjust colors and spacing, then move the final rules into the component's CSS file.

CODE
```jsx
// src/components/ProfileCard.jsx
import "./ProfileCard.css";

export function ProfileCard({ name, role, isOnline }) {
  return (
    <div className={`profile-card ${isOnline ? "online" : "offline"}`}>
      <h2>{name || "Unknown User"}</h2>
      {role && <p>{role}</p>}
      <p>{isOnline ? "Online" : "Offline"}</p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

```css
/* src/components/ProfileCard.css */
.profile-card {
  background: #151827;
  color: #f9fafb;
  padding: 16px 20px;
  margin: 10px 0;
  border-radius: 12px;
  border: 1px solid #292b3b;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
