KEY POINTS
- The key goes on <EnemyItem> inside map, not on the <li> inside the component.
- hp={enemy.hp} passes a number; quotes would pass a string.

IN PLAIN WORDS
State is data a component remembers between renders. useState gives you the current value and a setter function. Calling the setter tells React the value changed, and React redraws the component with the new value.

KEY IDEAS
1. import { useState } from "react"; gives the component access to state.
2. const [round, setRound] = useState(1);
   - round: the current value
   - setRound: the function that changes it
   - 1: the starting value
3. Pattern: const [value, setValue] = useState(initialValue);
4. onClick={handleNextRound} connects a button to a function. Pass the function itself, without ().

STEPS
What happens on a click:
1. The user clicks the button.
2. React runs handleNextRound.
3. The handler calls setRound(round + 1).
4. React re-renders Counter.
5. {round} shows the new value.

CODE
```jsx
import { useState } from "react";

function Counter() {
  const [round, setRound] = useState(1);

  function handleNextRound() {
    setRound(round + 1);
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Current round: {round}</p>
      <button onClick={handleNextRound}>Next round</button>
    </div>
  );
}

function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Enemy List</h1>
      <Counter />
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

KEY POINTS
- Other examples: [score, setScore] = useState(0), [xp, setXp] = useState(0).
- Only the setter updates the screen; changing round directly (round = 2) does nothing.
- onClick={handleNextRound()} would call it during render. Write onClick={handleNextRound}.

IN PLAIN WORDS
When the next state depends on the previous one, pass a function to the setter: setCount(prev => prev + 1). React calls it with the latest value, so several updates in a row all count.

KEY IDEAS
1. Value form: setCount(5) or setCount(count + 1). Fine when used once.
2. Functional updater: setCount(prev => prev + 1). Use it whenever the new value is built from the old one.
3. Two setCount(count + 1) calls in one handler add only 1, because both read the same snapshot.
4. Two setCount(prev => prev + 1) calls add 2: 0 → 1 → 2.
5. console.log(count) right after a setter still prints the old value.

CODE
```javascript
setCount(5);                 // fixed value
setCount(prev => prev + 1);  // based on the previous value
setHp(prev => prev - damage);
setScore(prev => prev + points);
setTurn(prev => prev + 1);
```

Wrong (ends at 1):
```javascript
function handleClick() {
  setCount(count + 1);
  setCount(count + 1);
}
```

Right (ends at 2):
```javascript
function handleClick() {
  setCount(prev => prev + 1);
  setCount(prev => prev + 1);
}
```

```javascript
function handleClick() {
  setCount(prev => prev + 1);
  console.log(count); // still the old value
}

function handleBonus() {
  setCoins(prev => prev + 3);
}
```

KEY POINTS
- The setter queues an update; it does not change the variable in the current handler.
- To add N in one step, use setX(prev => prev + N).

IN PLAIN WORDS
Never change a state object or array in place. Make a new copy with the change and pass the copy to the setter.

KEY IDEAS
1. Object: spread the old fields, then override the ones that change.
2. Add to an array: [...items, newItem].
3. Remove from an array: items.filter(...), which returns a new array.
4. React compares old and new state with Object.is. If it's the same object or array, React skips the re-render. A new copy is how React knows something changed.

CODE
Objects:
```javascript
// Wrong: changes the same object
player.hp = 80;
setPlayer(player);

// Right: new object
setPlayer({
  ...player,
  hp: player.hp + 10
});

setStats({
  ...stats,
  level: stats.level + 1,
  hp: stats.hp + 20,
  attack: stats.attack + 5
});
```

Arrays:
```javascript
// Wrong: changes the same array
items.push("Potion");
setItems(items);

// Right: add
setItems([...items, "Potion"]);

// Right: remove
setItems(items.filter(item => item !== "Shield"));
```

The arrow function in filter is short for:
```javascript
function (item) {
  return item !== "Shield";
}
```

KEY POINTS
- Mutating keeps the same reference, so React may not update the screen.
- Copying creates a new reference, so React re-renders.
- push, pop, splice and sort change the array in place; spread, filter and map return new arrays.

IN PLAIN WORDS
Handle a React form with onSubmit on the <form>, and call event.preventDefault() first. Keep errors in their own state object. On submit, collect errors; if there are any, show them and stop. Otherwise run the success code and reset the form.

KEY IDEAS
1. onSubmit goes on the <form>, not on the button.
2. event.preventDefault() stops the browser from reloading the page.
3. Inputs read and write state, not the DOM.
4. errors is an object, like { name: 'Name is required' }. Each field shows its error with &&.
5. Guard clause: if newErrors has any keys, set them and return early.

STEPS
handleSubmit:
1. event.preventDefault().
2. Make an empty newErrors object.
3. Check each field (trim() so spaces-only counts as empty) and add a message for each problem.
4. If Object.keys(newErrors).length > 0, call setErrors(newErrors) and return.
5. Otherwise run the success code.
6. Reset the form and clear the errors.

CODE
```jsx
const [form, setForm] = useState({ name: '', comment: '' });
const [errors, setErrors] = useState({});

function handleSubmit(event) {
  event.preventDefault();

  const newErrors = {};
  if (!form.name.trim()) {
    newErrors.name = 'Name is required';
  }
  if (!form.comment.trim()) {
    newErrors.comment = 'Comment is required';
  }

  if (Object.keys(newErrors).length > 0) {
    setErrors(newErrors);
    return;
  }

  console.log('Submit:', form);
  setForm({ name: '', comment: '' });
  setErrors({});
}
```

```jsx
<form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
  <input
    name="name"
    value={form.name}
    onChange={e => setForm({ ...form, [e.target.name]: e.target.value })}
  />
  {errors.name && <p className="error">{errors.name}</p>}
  {errors.comment && <p className="error">{errors.comment}</p>}
  <button type="submit">Submit</button>
</form>
```

KEY POINTS
- Success code runs only when there are no errors.
- Object.keys(obj).length counts how many keys an object has.

CORRECTION
The notes had an optional disabled={Object.keys(errors).length > 0} on the submit button. With that line, one failed submit disables the button for good, because errors only clear on a successful submit. Either leave it out (handleSubmit already blocks bad input) or clear the errors while the user types:
```jsx
onChange={e => {
  setForm({ ...form, [e.target.name]: e.target.value });
  setErrors({});
}}
```

IN PLAIN WORDS
When two or more components need the same data, keep that state in their closest common parent. The parent is the single source of truth: data goes down to children as props, and children send changes back up by calling functions the parent passed them.

KEY IDEAS
1. State lives in the closest common parent of everything that uses it.
2. Data flows down as props.
3. Events flow up through function props, like onAddSubmission.
4. Children display data and report events; only the parent updates the state.
5. Warning signs: two components each keep their own copy of the same thing, siblings show different values for it, or you find yourself syncing two values by hand.

STEPS
1. List the components that read this data.
2. Find their closest common parent.
3. Put the state there.
4. Pass the values down as props.
5. Pass handler functions down so children can ask for changes.
6. Update the state only in the parent.

CODE
```jsx
function HUD() {
  const [submissions, setSubmissions] = useState([]);

  function handleAddSubmission(newItem) {
    setSubmissions(prev => [...prev, newItem]);
  }

  return (
    <>
      <FeedbackForm onAddSubmission={handleAddSubmission} />
      <SubmissionsList submissions={submissions} />
    </>
  );
}

function FeedbackForm({ onAddSubmission }) {
  function handleSubmit(e) {
    e.preventDefault();
    const newItem = { text: 'example' }; // built from the inputs
    onAddSubmission(newItem);            // calls the parent's function
  }
  // ...inputs and <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
}
```

KEY POINTS
- Fix for duplicated state: move it up to the shared parent and pass data and callbacks down.
- Prop names for callbacks usually start with on (onAddSubmission, onSpawnEnemy).

IN PLAIN WORDS
Challenge: an Enemy Spawner. The form keeps formData as one object with a shared handleChange, validates with a guard clause, and has a Reset button. On a valid submit it calls onSpawnEnemy(enemy). App owns the enemies array (lifted state) and passes it to EnemyList, which maps it.

KEY IDEAS
1. EnemySpawnerForm owns formData and errors.
2. handleChange updates one field with [name]: value.
3. Health is typed as text, so it is converted with Number() and must be greater than 0.
4. App owns enemies and passes handleAddEnemy down as onSpawnEnemy.
5. EnemyList shows a message when the list is empty, and maps the enemies otherwise.
6. type="button" on Reset stops it from submitting the form.

STEPS
handleSubmit:
1. preventDefault().
2. Check name, type and health; collect errors.
3. If there are errors, show them and return.
4. Build the enemy object with trimmed text and a number for health.
5. Call onSpawnEnemy(enemy).
6. Clear the errors and reset the form.

CODE
```jsx
// EnemySpawnerForm.jsx
const [formData, setFormData] = useState({ name: "", type: "", health: "" });
const [errors, setErrors] = useState({});

function handleChange(e) {
  const { name, value } = e.target;
  setFormData((prev) => ({ ...prev, [name]: value }));
}

function handleSubmit(e) {
  e.preventDefault();

  const newErrors = {};
  if (!formData.name.trim()) newErrors.name = "Name is required";
  if (!formData.type.trim()) newErrors.type = "Type is required";

  const healthNumber = Number(formData.health);
  if (!healthNumber || healthNumber <= 0) {
    newErrors.health = "Health must be a positive number";
  }

  if (Object.keys(newErrors).length > 0) {
    setErrors(newErrors);
    return;
  }

  const enemy = {
    id: crypto.randomUUID(),
    name: formData.name.trim(),
    type: formData.type.trim(),
    health: healthNumber,
  };

  if (onSpawnEnemy) onSpawnEnemy(enemy);

  setErrors({});
  setFormData({ name: "", type: "", health: "" });
}

function handleReset() {
  setFormData({ name: "", type: "", health: "" });
  setErrors({});
}
```

```jsx
<form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
  <input name="name" value={formData.name} onChange={handleChange} />
  {errors.name && <p style={{ color: "red" }}>{errors.name}</p>}

  <input name="type" value={formData.type} onChange={handleChange} />
  {errors.type && <p style={{ color: "red" }}>{errors.type}</p>}

  <input type="number" name="health" value={formData.health} onChange={handleChange} />
  {errors.health && <p style={{ color: "red" }}>{errors.health}</p>}

  <button type="submit">Spawn Enemy</button>
  <button type="button" onClick={handleReset}>Reset</button>
</form>
```

```jsx
// App.jsx
function App() {
  const [enemies, setEnemies] = useState([]);

  function handleAddEnemy(enemy) {
    setEnemies((prev) => [...prev, enemy]);
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Controlled input demo</h1>
      <FeedbackForm />
      <EnemySpawnerForm onSpawnEnemy={handleAddEnemy} />
      <EnemyList enemies={enemies} />
    </div>
  );
}
```

```jsx
// EnemyList.jsx
export default function EnemyList({ enemies }) {
  if (enemies.length === 0) {
    return <p>No enemies spawned yet.</p>;
  }
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Enemy List</h2>
      <ul>
        {enemies.map((enemy) => (
          <li key={enemy.id}>
            <strong>{enemy.name}</strong> — {enemy.type} (HP: {enemy.health})
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
}
```

KEY POINTS
- Number("") is 0, so an empty health field fails the check.
- style={{ color: "red" }} is an inline style: the outer braces are JSX, the inner braces are a JavaScript object.

CORRECTION
The original EnemyList used key={index}, which Week 2, Lesson 5 says to avoid. The code above gives each enemy an id with crypto.randomUUID() when it's spawned and uses key={enemy.id}.

IN PLAIN WORDS
A side effect is work that isn't about drawing the UI: logging, saving data, timers, talking to a server. It doesn't belong in the render body, because the render body runs on every render. useEffect runs it after React has rendered; useEffect(fn, []) runs it once, after the component first appears (mounts).

KEY IDEAS
1. Code in the component body runs on every render. Heavy work there (saving, network calls) could run many times.
2. useEffect(() => { ... }) means "run this after render".
3. The [] is a setting, not data: run once after the first render.
4. Rules of Hooks: call hooks (useState, useEffect, ...) only at the top level of the component, above return. Never inside if statements, loops, JSX or nested functions.

CODE
Runs on every render (wrong place for side effects):
```jsx
function Test() {
  console.log('rendering Test');
  return <div>Test</div>;
}
```

Runs once after mount:
```jsx
import { useEffect } from "react";

function Test() {
  useEffect(() => {
    console.log('Side effect test mounted');
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Side effect</h2>
    </div>
  );
}
```

Not allowed:
```jsx
return <div>{useEffect(...)}</div>;   // inside JSX

if (something) {
  useEffect(...);                     // inside an if
}
```

KEY POINTS
- Render = describe the UI. Effect = work after the UI is on screen.
- useEffect(fn, []) is "when this component first shows up, run this once."

NOTE
In development, StrictMode runs a [] effect twice on mount (mount → clean up → mount again) to catch bugs, so "mounted" can log twice. In the built app it runs once.

IN PLAIN WORDS
To load data when a component appears: start state as an empty array, fetch inside useEffect(..., []), and put the JSON into state. React then re-renders with the data.

KEY IDEAS
1. useState([]) is a safe default: [].map() works and renders nothing. useState(null) would crash with "Cannot read properties of null (reading 'map')".
2. The effect callback can't be async. Define an async function inside it and call it.
3. After await res.json(), call the setter with the data.

STEPS
1. const [items, setItems] = useState([]);
2. In useEffect, write async function fetchItems() { ... }.
3. Inside it: await fetch(url), then await res.json().
4. setItems(json).
5. Call fetchItems() at the end of the effect.
6. Pass [] so it runs once on mount.
7. Render with items.map(...), each <li> with a key.

CODE
```jsx
import { useState, useEffect } from "react";

function SomeList() {
  const [items, setItems] = useState([]);

  useEffect(() => {
    async function fetchItems() {
      const res = await fetch("YOUR_URL_HERE");
      const json = await res.json();
      setItems(json);
    }
    fetchItems();
  }, []);

  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map((item) => (
        <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

KEY POINTS
- Three parts: useState([]), an async function defined and called inside useEffect(..., []), and setState(json).
- This version has no loading or error handling; Lesson 3 adds them.

WHY THE INNER FUNCTION
React expects an effect to return nothing or a cleanup function. An async function always returns a Promise, so the effect itself can't be async.

IN PLAIN WORDS
A fetch can be in one of four states: loading, error, empty or success. Track them with state, use try/catch/finally so every failure goes down one path and loading always turns off, and use early returns so exactly one state shows at a time.

KEY IDEAS
1. Loading: waiting for the response.
2. Error: the network failed or the server returned a bad status.
3. Empty: the fetch worked but returned no items.
4. Success: the fetch worked and there are items.
5. fetch only rejects on network failure. A 404 or 500 still resolves, so check response.ok and throw yourself.
6. finally runs whether the try succeeded or failed, so setLoading(false) always happens.

STEPS
In the effect:
1. setLoading(true) and setError(null).
2. try: fetch; if !response.ok, throw an Error with the status.
3. Parse JSON and save it to state.
4. catch: save the error.
5. finally: setLoading(false).

In the render:
1. if (loading) return the loading message.
2. if (error) return the error message.
3. if (enemies.length === 0) return the empty message.
4. Otherwise return the normal UI.

CODE
```jsx
const [enemies, setEnemies] = useState([]);
const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
const [error, setError] = useState(null);

useEffect(() => {
  async function fetchEnemies() {
    setLoading(true);
    setError(null);
    try {
      const response = await fetch("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos");
      if (!response.ok) {
        throw new Error(`HTTP error! status: ${response.status}`);
      }
      const json = await response.json();
      setEnemies(json);
    } catch (err) {
      setError(err);
    } finally {
      setLoading(false);
    }
  }
  fetchEnemies();
}, []);

if (loading) {
  return <div>Loading enemies...</div>;
}
if (error) {
  return <div>Failed to load enemies. Please try again later.</div>;
}
if (enemies.length === 0) {
  return <div>No enemies yet. Spawn some!</div>;
}
return (
  <div>
    <h1>Controlled input demo</h1>
    <FeedbackForm />
    <EnemySpawnerForm onSpawnEnemy={handleAddEnemy} />
    <EnemyList enemies={enemies} />
  </div>
);
```

KEY POINTS
- loading starts as true so the first render shows "Loading".
- Early returns run in order, so only one branch can show.
- By the third check, loading and error are already known to be false, so the extra !loading && !error from the original isn't needed.

IN PLAIN WORDS
The dependency array is the effect's watch list. With [mode], the effect runs after the first render and again every time mode changes. Putting mode in the fetch URL means every change of mode refetches.

KEY IDEAS
1. Mount = the first time a component appears on screen.
2. useEffect(fn, []): after mount only.
3. useEffect(fn, [value]): after mount, and after any render where value changed.
4. useEffect(fn): after every render (rarely right for a fetch).
5. The parent owns mode and passes it to the child as a prop.
6. A template literal (backticks with ${mode}) puts the current value into the URL.

STEPS
1. App: const [mode, setMode] = useState("all").
2. A button toggles mode between "all" and "hard".
3. App renders <EnemyFetcher mode={mode} />.
4. EnemyFetcher uses mode in the fetch URL.
5. The effect lists [mode], so each toggle runs the fetch again.

CODE
```jsx
