// Child
function EnemyFetcher({ mode }) {
  const [enemies, setEnemies] = useState([]);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    console.log("Effect running with mode:", mode);

    async function fetchEnemies() {
      setLoading(true);
      setError(null);
      try {
        const response = await fetch(
          `https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos?mode=${mode}`
        );
        if (!response.ok) {
          throw new Error(`HTTP error! status: ${response.status}`);
        }
        const json = await response.json();
        setEnemies(json);
      } catch (err) {
        setError(err);
      } finally {
        setLoading(false);
      }
    }

    fetchEnemies();
  }, [mode]);

  // loading / error / empty / success UI
}
```

KEY POINTS
- First load runs with "all"; each toggle runs the effect again with the new mode.
- Changing [mode] back to [] would fetch once and ignore later toggles.
- Read it as: "after rendering, if anything in this list changed, run the effect again."

NOTE
JSONPlaceholder doesn't have a "mode" field, so it sends back the same 200 todos either way. Toggling still proves the refetch happens (watch the console and the Network tab), but the data won't change.

IN PLAIN WORDS
A searchable list that fetches only when the user submits, not on every keystroke. liveQuery holds what's being typed; submittedQuery holds what was actually searched. Submitting copies live into submitted, and the effect watches submittedQuery, so it refetches only then.

KEY IDEAS
1. liveQuery: the controlled input's value; changes on every key.
2. submittedQuery: changes only on submit; this is what the fetch uses.
3. The parent (App) owns the search state and passes submittedQuery to the fetcher as query.
4. [mode, query] reruns the effect when either changes.
5. encodeURIComponent(query) makes spaces and symbols safe inside a URL.

STEPS
1. The user types; liveQuery updates.
2. The user presses Search or Enter; handleSearchSubmit runs.
3. preventDefault(), then setSubmittedQuery(liveQuery).
4. query changes, so the effect runs and fetches.
5. The four states show loading, then error, empty or success.

CODE
